# BrandConfig & White-Label DI Conventions

## Contracts live in `core:domain` — no framework deps

`BrandConfig` and `FeatureToggles` are plain Kotlin in `core/domain`. Never import Metro, Compose, or Android APIs there.

## Every client module follows the same pattern

```kotlin
@ContributesBinding(AppScope::class)
@Inject
class AcmeBrandConfig : BrandConfig {
    override val appName = "Acme"
    override val apiBaseUrl = "https://api.acme.com"
    override val supportEmail = "support@acme.com"
    override val primaryColorArgb = 0xFFE53935L
    override val onPrimaryColorArgb = 0xFFFFFFFFL
    override val features = FeatureToggles(hasHvacCertifications = true)
}
```

- Both `@ContributesBinding(AppScope::class)` and `@Inject` are required — Metro won't wire the binding without them.
- The `replaces` parameter overrides another binding: `@ContributesBinding(AppScope::class, replaces = [DefaultBrandConfig::class])`.

## The Metro graph lives in `sharedUI`

`AppGraph` in `sharedUI/src/commonMain/kotlin/com/velsol/di/AppGraph.kt` is the only `@DependencyGraph`. Platform entry points call `createGraph<AppGraph>()` to get an instance; Metro picks up all `@ContributesBinding(AppScope::class)` declarations from the classpath.

Never create a second `@DependencyGraph` — all bindings funnel through `AppGraph`.

## Consuming brand config in Compose

Use `LocalBrandConfig.current` — never pass `BrandConfig` as a parameter through the composable tree:

```kotlin
// ✅ GOOD — read from CompositionLocal
val features = LocalBrandConfig.current.features
if (features.hasHvacCertifications) EpaFormButton()

// ❌ BAD — threading config through every composable
fun SomeScreen(config: BrandConfig) { ... }
```

`LocalBrandConfig` is provided by `AppTheme`; it is always present inside `App()`.

## Adding a new feature flag

1. Add the property to `FeatureToggles` in `core/domain` with a `false` default.
2. Override it in whichever client modules need it set to `true`.
3. The compiler enforces no client can be missing the field.

## Active client is a Gradle property

`settings.gradle.kts` and `sharedUI/build.gradle.kts` both read `-Pclient=xxx` (default: `default`). The selected client module is wired in at configuration time — never at runtime.
